The following expression connects the number of events in a particular kinematic bin $\Delta^{4}\tau = \Delta\alpha_{s} \Delta t \Delta \phi \Delta p_{st}$ with the differential cross section of the investigated process in the center of this bin. 

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\Delta N (\Delta^{4}\tau ) = \mathcal{L} \cdot \frac{\textrm{d}^{4}\sigma}{p_{\textrm{st}}\textrm{d}p_{\textrm{st}}\textrm{d}\alpha_{s}\textrm{d}t \textrm{d}\phi } \cdot p_{st} \cdot \Delta \alpha_{s} \cdot \Delta t \cdot \Delta \phi \cdot \Delta p_{st} \cdot T.
\end{aligned}\label{eq:n_events}
\end{equation}
Here $\mathcal{L}$ is the luminosity of the experiment, $\Delta \alpha_{s}$, $\Delta t$, $\Delta \phi$, and $\Delta p_{st}$ are the bin widths in all four kinematic variables, and $T$ is the duration of the experiment. In Eq.~\eqref{eq:n_events}, $\alpha_{s}$ is considered to be equal to unity, and therefore is not present in the numerator of the expression for the differential cross section.

\newpage

%$\Delta N_{\sigma} = N_{CT3} -N_{GEA}$,   $f=\frac{\Delta N_{\sigma}}{N_{GEA}}$

From the theoretical perspective, $\sigma_{GEA}$ corresponds to the cross section calculated within the generalized eikonal approximation without color transparency effects, while $\sigma_{CT3}$ corresponds to the case when contributions from the color transparency effects were included in the framework of the quantum diﬀusion model with the free parameter $\Delta M^{2}=3$~GeV$^{2}$ (see Refs.~\cite{Frankfurt:1996uz,PhysRevC.107.014605,Larionov:2025equ} for details). Therefore, for the proposed experiment, one needs to estimate the statistics that would allow one to distinguish between $\sigma_{GEA}$ and $\sigma_{CT3}$ on a stage of theoretical analysis in order to make a decisive conclusion regarding CT contributions.


To perform this estimation, it is convenient to introduce the following variables, $\varepsilon$ -- the accuracy with which the absolute difference between $\sigma_{GEA}$ and $\sigma_{CT3}$ is proposed to be measured and $f=\frac{N_{CT3} -N_{GEA}}{N_{GEA}}=\frac{\Delta N_{\sigma}}{N_{GEA}}$ -- the relative difference between the corresponding cross sections. Then the following derivations can be performed in order to estimate the desired statistics.

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\sqrt{N_{\varepsilon}} & = && \varepsilon \cdot \Delta N_{\sigma} \\
\sqrt{N_{\varepsilon}} & = && \varepsilon \cdot f \cdot  N_{\varepsilon}\\
1 & = && \varepsilon \cdot f \cdot \sqrt{N_{\varepsilon}}\\
N_{\varepsilon} &=&& \frac{1}{\varepsilon^{2} \cdot f^{2}}
\end{aligned}\label{eq:n_evt_eps} 
\end{equation}

From Tab.~\ref{tab:event_per_year}, for $p_{st} = 0.2$~GeV/c, the relative difference between the cross section values can be estimated as $f = 0.6$. Therefore, according to Eq.~\eqref{eq:n_evt_eps}, for the accuracy $\varepsilon$ = 20\%, the desired number of events $N_{\varepsilon} = 69$. This would correspond to about 129 days of continuous data-taking for $\mathcal{L} = 2.7\cdot 10^{30}\textrm{cm}^{-2}\textrm{s}^{-1}$ and about 35 days of continuous data-taking for $\mathcal{L} = 10^{31}\textrm{cm}^{-2}\textrm{s}^{-1}$.

\newpage

\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
&F = [\mathcal{L}]\times [\mu \textrm{b}\rightarrow \textrm{cm}^{2}] \times [\Delta \alpha_{s} \Delta t \Delta \phi \Delta p_{st}] \times [\textrm{time}]\\[20pt]
&F = \left[2.7\cdot 10^{30}\textrm{cm}^{-2}\textrm{s}^{-1}\right] \times \left[10^{-30}\textrm{cm}^{2}\right] \times \left[ 0.2 \cdot 2.5 \cdot 1.047 \cdot 0.04 \right] \times \left[10^{7} s\right] = 565.38\cdot 10^{3}\\[20pt]
\end{aligned}\label{eq:rrr}
\end{equation*}

%24 \cdot 60 \cdot 60 \cdot 365~\textrm{s}

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
&p_{st}=0.2~\textrm{GeV/c}\\[20pt]
&\frac{\textrm{d}^{4}\sigma}{p_{\textrm{st}}\textrm{d}p_{\textrm{st}}\textrm{d}\alpha_{s}\textrm{d}t \textrm{d}\phi }= 1.0\cdot 10^{-3} \mu \textrm{b} /\textrm{GeV}^{4} ~~~\left[ \textrm{at}~~p_{st} = 0.2~\textrm{GeV/c},~\textrm{for}~CT3 \right]\\[20pt]
&N = \frac{\textrm{d}^{4}\sigma}{p_{\textrm{st}}\textrm{d}p_{\textrm{st}}\textrm{d}\alpha_{s}\textrm{d}t \textrm{d}\phi } \times p_{st} \times F = 1.0\cdot 10^{-3}\times 0.2 \times 565.38\cdot 10^{3} = \textcolor{red}{\bf 113}
\end{aligned}\label{eq:rrr}
\end{equation*}

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
&p_{st}=0.1~\textrm{GeV/c}\\[20pt]
&\frac{\textrm{d}^{4}\sigma}{p_{\textrm{st}}\textrm{d}p_{\textrm{st}}\textrm{d}\alpha_{s}\textrm{d}t \textrm{d}\phi }= 50.3\cdot 10^{-3} \mu \textrm{b} /\textrm{GeV}^{4} ~~~\left[ \textrm{at}~~p_{st} = 0.1~\textrm{GeV/c},~\textrm{for}~CT3 \right]\\[20pt]
&N = \frac{\textrm{d}^{4}\sigma}{p_{\textrm{st}}\textrm{d}p_{\textrm{st}}\textrm{d}\alpha_{s}\textrm{d}t \textrm{d}\phi } \times p_{st} \times F = 50.3\cdot 10^{-3}\times 0.1 \times 565.38 \cdot 10^{3} =  \textcolor{red}{\bf 2844}
\end{aligned}\label{eq:rrr}
\end{equation*}


